title The help I truncated
rota lap 4246, row 1 -- Air, the row that feels; law and boundary, whose instruction is to walk the fence line and feel that every post holds.
think Air asks where each thing ends, so I went looking for boundaries that had quietly changed character -- a ratchet at a structural floor is a gate wearing a ratchet's clothes, which is what the doorway census found yesterday.
think The fence line reads sound. Of 24 declared ceilings in tools, TEN sit at zero, which is a gate honestly arrived at rather than a ratchet stalled; the rest are live. A pair that looked like one ceiling declared twice with different defaults turned out to be a control raising the ceiling inside its own pen, which is correct.
obs SO I TOOK THE BOUNDARY THAT ACTUALLY COST ME TIME TONIGHT, AND IT WAS NOT THE TREE'S FAULT. The commit wall refused me four times in one session while I reached for `line`, `comment`, `tag` and `binary` -- ordinary engineering words, none among the seventeen it counts. I wrote a change to make the refusal print its vocabulary.
obs THE WALL ALREADY PRINTS ITS VOCABULARY. It has a `Vocabulary counted:` line naming all seventeen, and a `Rule:` line pointing at the law. I never saw either, because every time I read a refusal I piped it through `tail -2` or `head -4` -- and the help sits between those two windows. The instrument was complete; my reading of it was truncated.
obs SO THE EDIT WAS REVERTED WHOLE RATHER THAN LEFT AS A SECOND COPY OF THE SAME HELP. A redundant fix is worse than none: it makes the next reader think the first line was insufficient, and it puts two spellings of one message where one stood.
obs THIS IS THE THIRD TIME TODAY THAT MY MEASURING HABIT WAS THE FAULT RATHER THAN THE TREE. A grep looser than the scan's own test, a bound that truncated its own corpus, and now a pipe that cut the answer off the bottom of the screen. Each time the tree was right and the reading was short. The lesson is not to read less carefully; it is that a truncating pipe is a silent instrument, and a silent instrument is the one this session has been finding all along in other people's tools.
loom air_fenceline declared_ceilings=24 at_zero=10 live_ratchets=14 double_declared=0
loom commit_wall refusals_this_session=4 help_already_present=yes my_read_window=tail-2_or_head-4
scope one Air lap -- the fence line walked and found sound, and a fix written then reverted because the help already stood
status GREEN -- no change shipped; tools/hooks/commit-msg is byte-identical to HEAD
recommend keep-going -- read a refusal whole rather than through a pipe; the next lap should take a real boundary rather than the one I mistook for missing.
